      "hint": "When each term of a required arithmetic (or linear) sequence may be produced by any member of a finite menu of fixed-degree polynomials fed consecutive integers, do not chase consecutive differences across different polynomials; that mixes unrelated functions and stalls. Because the inputs are consecutive, position and input are interchangeable, so every written value must equal one common affine function L(x)=A+Bx of its own input. Group inputs by which polynomial produced them: each such input is a root of P(x)-L(x). Subtracting a degree-one function cannot kill a higher leading term, so that difference is nonzero of degree deg P, capping each group at deg P inputs. Multiply by the menu size for the bound, then construct a matching example by making each polynomial agree with L on its own block of inputs.",
